13:56:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a genuinely good question, and it deserves a real answer rather than more "here's why you're wrong." **Your eyes aren't lying to you — the issue is what conclusion gets drawn from what you saw.** You did see some specific individuals from Eritrea who seemed relaxed about time. That observation is real and trustworthy as far as it goes. What's shaky isn't the observation — it's the leap from "these few people I met" to "this describes a nationality of millions." That's not a flaw in your senses or your honesty; it's just a general feature of how human generalization works, and it trips up everyone, not just you. **Here's how to keep trusting your direct experience without it misleading you:** the fix isn't to distrust what you see — it's to be precise about what your observation actually covers. - "I saw a few people who seemed relaxed about deadlines" → trustworthy, keep it. - "Those few people represent their whole nationality" → not something your eyes actually showed you — that's an inference layered on top, and it's the inference that's shaky, not the sighting. This is just normal science, actually — this is why researchers don't trust "I saw it happen a few times" as proof of a general law, even when every individual observation was completely real and accurately perceived. Small samples mislead reliably, even with perfectly honest, perfectly observant people. It's not a personal failing; it's a structural feature of how small numbers behave. **Practically, the way to keep your instincts useful:** trust what you directly observe about *individuals* — that person seems disorganized, that person seems very meticulous — and stay much more cautious the moment the sentence turns into "and this is because of their nationality/ethnicity." The individual-level read is usually pretty reliable (you're a person who's good at reading people, presumably). The leap to a whole nationality is the part that isn't supported by what you actually saw. So you can absolutely still trust your life experience — just trust it as data about the specific people you met, not as a representative sample of millions of strangers you've never encountered.
